1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,270
Why can't you tickle yourself?

2
00:00:02,500 --> 00:00:05,185
Someone else's finger can make you squirm.

3
00:00:05,205 --> 00:00:08,905
Your own finger, same spot, feels dull.

4
00:00:09,025 --> 00:00:12,325
That's because your brain predicts its own touch.

5
00:00:12,419 --> 00:00:15,539
Every time you move, it sends a copy of the command,

6
00:00:15,599 --> 00:00:19,619
a forecast of what you'll feel, and turns that feeling down.

7
00:00:19,821 --> 00:00:22,719
In 1998, brain scans showed it:

8
00:00:22,739 --> 00:00:25,699
the touch area reacted less to people's own touch

9
00:00:25,719 --> 00:00:27,701
than to one they didn't make.

10
00:00:27,721 --> 00:00:30,001
Then the team got clever.

11
00:00:30,081 --> 00:00:33,185
People moved a robot arm that stroked their own palm.

12
00:00:33,205 --> 00:00:36,525
Add a delay of a fifth of a second, or twist the direction,

13
00:00:36,545 --> 00:00:39,565
and the same touch felt more ticklish.

14
00:00:39,623 --> 00:00:40,823
The forecast missed,

15
00:00:40,843 --> 00:00:43,543
so the brain treated it as the outside world.

16
00:00:43,974 --> 00:00:46,854
So a tickle is really about surprise.

17
00:00:47,194 --> 00:00:49,914
Your brain feels what it didn't see coming.
